; Every dependency of this package is public, and this file says so out loud
; rather than leaving it to whatever registry the machine happens to be pointed
; at. That is not a preference: `npm install` writes a `resolved` URL per package
; into package-lock.json, so the configured registry is committed along with it.
; On a work machine the configured registry is a corporate mirror, and several
; hundred of its URLs reached this public repository once — an internal hostname
; published, and a CI that then timed out fetching from a host a GitHub runner
; has no way to resolve. The failure surfaced on one Node version as a plain
; ETIMEDOUT and on the other as "Exit handler never called!", which is a long
; way from the cause.
;
; What this covers, precisely, because the loose version of the claim is wrong:
;
;   - It pins the DEFAULT registry. `@scope:registry` is a separate config key,
;     not an override of this one, and npm consults it first for a scoped
;     package. So a user-level `@scope:registry` pointing at a mirror still wins
;     for that scope, and this project installs several scoped packages.
;     Enumerating them here would only move the problem: the list would go stale
;     the first time a scoped dependency is added, and a stale list reads as a
;     guarantee it no longer gives.
;   - It governs GENERATION only. `npm ci` installs from the `resolved` URLs
;     already in the lockfile, so a lockfile written on a machine without this
;     pin is not corrected by having it.
;
; Both gaps land in the same place: scripts/hygiene.mjs checks every `resolved`
; URL in the lockfile against an allow list, whatever scope it belongs to and
; wherever the file was written. That check is the guarantee. This file is the
; part that keeps it from having to fire.
;
; npm never packs .npmrc into a published tarball, and "files" in package.json
; does not list it either, so nothing here ships.
registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/
